<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harput</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kharpert</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harput is a historic hill town in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, just north of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elazig">Elazığ</a>. Its castle commanded the plain of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and was held from the early 12th century by an Artuqid branch, whose ruler Belek imprisoned King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin II of Jerusalem</a> there in 1123. Its population gradually moved down to the new town of Elazığ in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harput.jpg" alt="Harput" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rev. Edwin M. Bliss, Turkey and the Armenian Atrocities, London, 1896 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elaz%C4%B1%C4%9F_54.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
